    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand your need for a serious, professional training guide without the distractions. You are absolutely right to focus on the legal risks and procedural integrity.\n\nSkipping straight to a written warning without a prior verbal warning is a significant legal pitfall. The primary risk is that the employee may claim they were never properly notified of the issue or the specific expectations that were not met. Without the initial verbal conversation, you cannot prove that you gave the employee a fair opportunity to correct the behavior before escalating the discipline. This can lead to claims of wrongful termination or unfair labor practices, especially if the employee argues they were unaware of the problem until the written warning was served.\n\nAdditionally, if the employee contests the termination later, the lack of a documented verbal warning step creates a gap in your chain of evidence. It becomes difficult to demonstrate that the employer followed a consistent, progressive process designed to give the employee a chance to improve. Courts and arbitrators often look for this pattern of escalating discipline as proof that the employer acted in good faith and followed established procedures.\n\nTo mitigate these risks, it is standard practice to document the verbal warning immediately after the conversation, noting the date, time, location, the specific issues discussed, and the agreed-upon corrective actions.",
